91 :question/id #uuid "6d46c442-4675-5e0e-a834-a8b10ad2b86a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4424" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5,$ $R_6:= 4 R_6,$ and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7.$ What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e01723b-cc89-5e3f-b933-6517b2094770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409662} {:answer/id #uuid "91ff05b8-c923-5cbc-9974-2858e86d4877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409672} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b3cea5-79ed-5121-8909-27b79eb945a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409674} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a98397-80e5-5a87-b4ed-d166d96908c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409675} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7338ba-732f-5bfb-aded-530b4a5f8d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc16361c-3306-5054-a003-e31388cab6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409687}] :question/id #uuid "0a9d752b-69e4-5f21-888f-3429a8443f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42503" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcd0ef5-cddc-5b14-a762-3186a55411ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327050} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3eb04d-be6d-5348-bdb8-adaed96c8237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327868} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef137e2-5781-5f7e-a21a-a90c9f39bd53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328179} {:answer/id #uuid "ab28b1f0-721f-5a2e-9375-c5d915acae43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328413} {:answer/id #uuid "b566a19c-1d6f-53e9-be6e-d7a83555acb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327868} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1c6017-728a-5a92-b5db-59229be84cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329545}] :question/id #uuid "e5191b0f-c325-5a66-9045-15afd6131a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-42505" :question/problem "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=10.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{2},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{2}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0f29de-25aa-5107-8046-0dd732f9beb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} {:answer/id #uuid "162deb0b-2730-571d-b037-7db47a3aea46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263209} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8f75e2-26ce-5967-ac8a-f06080423598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263259} {:answer/id #uuid "66de9e61-f678-5f09-92fa-20666c0a4a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263891} {:answer/id #uuid "f578aa5a-7ba5-5a91-8942-93f38512c9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00b119d-2bbd-5aff-88de-546820f3c832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265676}] :question/id #uuid "da7f3dfc-3649-55d6-80af-942a6a83828d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130767" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=28.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{3}{4},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{3}{4}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{3}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c504d34-49a8-5293-9654-9bb371251af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263118} {:answer/id #uuid "668e2da3-ac3d-5bec-b6b1-16504c2d81fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263886} {:answer/id #uuid "7573103e-73e5-56e6-832d-af6582780369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264021} {:answer/id #uuid "90344634-d8cd-51fb-85d7-7f08481a8bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} {:answer/id #uuid "c168c56a-3c78-5c71-873d-48190da505aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} :answer-field/id #uuid "37455212-8943-576e-a134-59b320488bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265345}] :question/id #uuid "b71bc5d0-3557-578c-8a3d-25da59fcce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-130771" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-75.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{4}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-75) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ceb8d39-892a-568d-bb1d-1e0de0abcd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327183} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2ad961-98aa-51c8-9cf6-358e73cf2d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327204} {:answer/id #uuid "6aec1ceb-3826-5f48-a5ff-67e8e8026011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327855} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ed0c5c-a2b6-593c-825e-2e9391bcf551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328911} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ad7918-1d64-5a4a-9ddf-ab50ee7931ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327204} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1229dc0-d6f0-51b6-b46e-d6021bec8ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329522}] :question/id #uuid "cd8840c4-6eae-5436-8938-81828d23c8b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42204" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=4.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038f903c-07cf-5804-8ccf-c48c4437fe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326971} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5d0fbb-4c6d-516a-bbe1-bc9a4754768c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327766} {:answer/id #uuid "7edb508d-7daa-5a42-80ea-